52

Plants contamination by toxic metals
Fir needles were found to be the most susceptible
to contamination by the tested metals, while the
most uncontaminated plant parts were the aerial
parts of rapeseed (Table 1). Moreover, the
contamination of plants by toxic metals was not
dependent on the development intensity of the
region, the differences between the E and U
regions were insignificant (p>0.05). The high
concentration of Pb, Al, Mn and Ni in the fir
needles could be a result of their effective transfer
from soil as well as atmospheric deposition
(Gandois & Probst, 2012). Moreover, the presence
of sticky wax on the surface of the needles could
act as a glue for pollutants (Gostin, 2010).
However, the exact cause of such high fir needle
contamination is difficult to assess. Taking into
consideration the comparable Cd content in soil
samples from the E and U regions indicates that
the lower Cd content in fir needles in the E region
is not associated with the purity of the soil but
more likely with air pollution. The specific
susceptibility to heavy metal contamination by
Abies alba needles has been confirmed in the
studies of Gandois and Probst, (2012), who tested
levels of Al and Ni similar to those of the present
study, and slightly lower levels of Cd, Pb and Mn.
Among the melliferous plants, for goldenrod
(Solidago) a particularly high accumulation of
cadmium was found. However, the differences
between samples collected from the E and U
regions were not significant (p>0.05). It was
confirmed that the common dandelion (Taraxacum
officinale) is a lead concentrator (p<0.05). Despite
the high Cd content found in the examined samples
of goldenrod, the results of the present research are
much lower than those found by Tóth et al. (2012),
who tested the level of Cd in goldenrod leaves
from Hungary and found it to be about 1.4 mg/kg
d.w. In turn, common dandelion is considered to be
a useful reference point in heavy metal
environmental monitoring, due to the fact that it is
a widespread species in all temperate regions of the
world, as a ubiquitous wild plant of gardens,
meadows, lawns, road-sides, and ruderal habitats,
often growing on heavily polluted sites, such as
railway tracks or in the dusty surroundings of
industrial plants (Ligocki et al., 2011). Studies
have shown that among the examined plants, the
dandelion is characterized by an especially high
content of Cd, Al and Ni, which indicates the
susceptibility of this plant to the accumulation of
the aforementioned elements. Kajka and
Rutkowska (2018) tested for toxic metals in the
above ground parts of dandelion which grow near
roads with a high traffic intensity in central Poland

and found significantly higher levels of Ni (4-7
mg/kg d.w.) and concentrations of Cd and Pb
comparable to the present study (0.3 – 1 and 0.15
to 0.7 mg/kg d.w., respectively). On the other
hand, Ligocki et al. (2011) tested the metal content
in the leaves of dandelion from the urbanized and
village regions in the north part of Poland and
found levels significantly lower than the present
study of Cd, Pb, and Ni and comparable to the
present study concentrations of Mn. However,
similarly to the present study, authors did not find
any significant differences between the samples
collected from differently polluted regions.
In the literature Brassica napus is known to exhibit
a tendency to accumulate metals, especially toxic
ones (Angelova et al., 2017). The authors assert
that rapeseed roots are specific accumulators of
toxic metals, whereas the aboveground parts do not
exhibit such strong properties. Yu et al. (2012)
reported of Brassica napus that the aboveground
parts had a comparable level of Cd to the present
study (0.168 mg/kg d.m.) while higher levels of Pb
and Ni were present (0.416 and 0.76 mg/kg d.m.,
respectively) On the other hand, Niedźwiecka and
Zamorska-Wojdyła (2017) did not detect Pb in
Brassica napus growing near power plants.Tilia, as
the only leafy tree tested was characterized by a
relatively low content of the analysed elements,
and was the only plant where Ni was not detected.
Similar observations have been made by
Tomašević et al. (2004). In turn, Šijacic-Nikolic et
al. (2012) found in the leaves of tilia trees growing
along the highway significantly higher levels of Ni
and Pb (0.52-2.91 and 1.14-6.27 mg/kg d.m.,
respectively) and lower levels of Mn (12.12-74.87
mg/kg d.m.) as compared to the present study. The
same authors stated that the contents of trace
elements in tilia leaves are primarily influenced by
the genotype when they grow in the same soil
conditions.
The variable concentrations of heavy metals in
different plant species indicate the different
absorption abilities of pollutants from the
environment (Kajka and Rutkowska, 2018). It was
confirmed by Filipović-Trajković et al. (2012) that
based on metal distribution in plant organs, it is
possible to assess heavy metal migration from soil
to plant. Moreover, Petryk (2016) indicated that
the intensity of element uptake and release by
plants is significantly affected by soil composition.
Bee body contamination with toxic metals
Bees can come into contact with toxic metals not
only through contaminated water, but also during
the collection of contaminated nectar and pollen
from plants (Van der Steen et al. 2012; Costa et al.,

Acta Universitatis Cibiniensis Series E: FOOD TECHNOLOGY
Vol. XXIV (2020), no. 1

53

2019). A similar concentration of tested metals was
found in bee bodies collected from urbanized (U)
and ecological (E) regions (p>0.05; Table 2). In the
opposite case, Sadowska et al. (2019) found that in
contrast to Pb, Cd concentration in bee bodies was
dependent on environmental contamination. The
concentration of Cd found in bees from the
Podkarpackie region was comparable to the results
obtained by Fakhimzadeh and Lodenius (2000) for
Finnish bees (0.05–1.2 mg/kg) but significantly
higher as compared to Costa et al. (2019; 0.008–
0.195 mg/kg), or Perugini et al. (2011; 0.05–0.06
mg/kg) for Italian bees. The content of Pb in tested
bee bodies was comparable to the findings of
Porrini et al. (2002) for bees inhabiting the Italian
national park (0.15–0.55 mg/kg d.m.), and other
non-contaminated sites of Italy (0.25–1.40 mg/kg
d.m.; Costa et al., 2019; Conti and Botre, 2001) as
well as in the urban and industrial sites of Belgium
(0.33–0.41 mg/kg d.m.; Van Der Steen et al.,
2015). Roman (2010) measured cadmium in bee
bodies from the industrialized areas of Poland in
the range of 1.46 to 2.32 mg/kg d.m. Thus,
compared to the cited authors, the results obtained
for Pb in the present study are in compliance with
the data for environmentally pure bees habitats.
The Mn content in the tested bees was very
variable.

As compared to the literature, the average content
in the tested bees was found to be higher than in
the study conducted in Poland by Skorbiłowicz et
al. (2018) who found a level of 49.4 mg/kg d.m. in
bees collected in June, and 26.6 mg.kg d.m. in bees
collected in March. In the study carried out by
Zhelyazkova (2012) in Bulgaria (in the vicinity of
Stara Zagora), the concentration of Mn in the
analysed bees varied between 43-114 mg/kg d.m.
while in the study of Zarić et al. (2016) in Serbia
the Mn concentrations ranged from 34 to 90 mg/kg
d.m. The results obtained are comparable with our
previous study focused on the transfer of heavy
metals from bees to honey (Dżugan et al., 2018).
Honey contamination by toxic metals
As the last link in the food chain, honey was tested
for the concentration of toxic metals. As expected,
our results indicated different levels of toxic metals
depending on the honey variety, plant species and
region of collection (E and U; Table 2). Moreover,
significant differences in the content of particular
elements, which were measured in the same honey
variety but with different origins were observed. A
similar tendency during the testing of heavy metals
in different types of Polish honey was reported
(Sitarz-Palczak et al., 2015; Dżugan et al., 2017;
2018).

